{define: Asus4 base-fret 1 frets x 0 2 2 3 0}
{define: DaddA base-fret 1 frets x 0 0 2 3 5} 
{define: D- base-fret 1 frets x x 0 0 3 2} 
{define: Dsus2 base-fret 1 frets 0 0 0 2 3 0} 
{define: Dsus4 base-fret 1 frets x x 0 2 3 3} 
{define: Em7 base-fret 1 frets 0 2 2 0 3 0} 
{define: F#m base-fret 1 frets x x 4 2 2 2} 
{t: MAGGIE MAY - Rod Stewart }
{st: (Quittenton/Stewart) }
{artist: Rod Stewart }
{composer: Quittenton; Stewart }


https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pj2EQRLm7Vc  (Capo 0)

TIP:  If transposing, play the root chord of the transposed (DaddA) and add the 5th note as necessary.

TIP:  In the intro, go from (Em) to (Em7) but just adding your pinky to the 2nd string/3rd fret.  At the end of the first part you strum (G) and then three individual strums of |G| |D| and |D-| in which you just lift off the 6th string.

TIP:  You can just play the chords but here's how to strum the notes of the mandolin part:  Strum (D) four times.  Pull off the 1st string for one strum of (Dsus2), back on for one strum of (D).  Add pinky to 1st string/3rd fret for one strum of (Dsus4), now still holding the (D) shape extend the pinky over to the 5th fret for four strums of (DaddA).  Now go backwards:  pinky back down to the 3rd fret for (Dsus4), lift off for (D), lift off the 1st finger for (Dsus2), now jump to (G) for four strums, back to (D), lift off the 1st string again for two quick strums of (Dsus2), finish with strums of (D).  Repeat as indicated.  The key is to hit that first (Dsus2) in the sequence.  Practice it a few times, not too difficult, just looks messy.


